  "instance_name": "historical_context_15th_century_rats",
  "label": "European rats in the 15th century",
  "description": "The phrase 'European rats in the 15th century' refers to the historical context of the Black Death (bubonic plague) pandemic, which ravaged Europe between 1347 and 1351. While the plague was primarily spread by fleas on infected rats (specifically the black rat, Rattus rattus), the meme uses this historical association to imply that rats are a harbinger of catastrophic human extinction. The 15th century is also a period of significant societal upheaval, including the aftermath of the plague, the rise of the Renaissance, and the beginning of the Age of Exploration — all of which are implicitly referenced by the historical framing.",
